About the World Meteorological Organization

The World Meteorological Organization is a specialized agency of the United Nations and the UN system’s authoritative voice on weather, climate and water.

WMO supports international cooperation in meteorology, climatology, operational hydrology and related geophysical sciences. It provides a framework through which its 193 Member States and Territories exchange Earth-system information and strengthen services that protect lives, livelihoods, economies and the environment.

Its Secretariat is headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland. Learn more about WMO’s mandate.

This institutional environment makes the internship particularly relevant for candidates seeking to understand how a UN specialized agency develops partnerships beyond governments. The intern will gain exposure to WMO’s engagement with companies, foundations, investors, philanthropies, academic organizations and other non-state stakeholders.

About the Private Sector and Philanthropic Engagement Unit

The Private Sector and Philanthropic Engagement Unit operates within WMO’s Division of Development Partnerships and Programme Delivery.

The Unit coordinates WMO’s institutional engagement with private and non-state actors, including:

Private-sector companies;

Philanthropic foundations and philanthropists;

Commercial entities and technology companies;

Business and industry associations;

Investors and prospective contributors;

High-net-worth individuals; and

Other organizations capable of contributing finance, expertise, technology or in-kind resources.

The Unit’s responsibility extends beyond identifying donors. It cultivates high-impact partnerships and supports WMO technical departments and Members in pursuing appropriate, mutually beneficial cooperation with external organizations.

That work requires accurate research, effective relationship management, sound documentation, reputational safeguards and an understanding of WMO’s public-interest mandate.

WMO describes public-private engagement as cooperation among public institutions, businesses, academia and civil society in producing and delivering weather, climate, hydrological, marine and environmental information and services. Its approach emphasizes public value, integrity, mutual benefit, transparency, sovereignty and sustainability. Read about WMO’s public-private engagement framework.

The WMO Commons

The WMO Commons is WMO’s pooled financing mechanism. According to the vacancy announcement, it aims to mobilize USD 100 million over five years.

The intern will help the Unit identify potential contributors and strategic partners, conduct background research and maintain materials used for contributor and investor engagement.

This work will provide practical experience in international resource mobilization, including:

Identifying potential contributors;

Understanding organizational funding priorities;

Assessing strategic alignment;

Preparing cases for support;

Developing partner profiles;

Updating pitch materials; and

Recording and following up on engagement.

The Open Consultative Platform

The Open Consultative Platform is WMO’s principal mechanism for structured dialogue with private-sector and academic stakeholders.

It supports discussion about partnerships, innovation and the future of the global weather, climate and water enterprise. The intern will assist with the preparation and follow-up of platform sessions and other high-level engagements.

Assignments may include preparing concept notes, researching participants, developing agendas, compiling briefing packages, coordinating participation, recording meeting outcomes and monitoring follow-up actions.

Why WMO Is Recruiting for This Placement

The Private Sector and Philanthropic Engagement Unit is managing a growing portfolio connected with the WMO Commons, the Open Consultative Platform and wider partnership-development work.

At the same time, the Unit is preparing for several prominent international engagements, including:

TECO Amsterdam in October 2026;

The fourth session of WMO’s Services Commission, known as SERCOM-4;

COP31 in Antalya in November 2026; and

The World Economic Forum in January 2027.

The vacancy explains that the volume and complexity of these workstreams exceed the Unit’s current staffing capacity.

The selected intern will provide operational support in research, meeting coordination, documentation, due diligence and communications. Candidates should therefore expect a fast-moving environment involving several simultaneous assignments and deadlines.

Detailed Duties and Responsibilities

1. Partnership engagement and meeting support

The intern will help coordinate bilateral meetings involving companies, philanthropic foundations, investors and other non-state actors.

Responsibilities will include:

Preparing meeting agendas;

Researching organizations and participants;

Developing background notes and participant profiles;

Taking accurate meeting notes;

Converting discussions into structured summaries;

Recording decisions and agreed actions;

Following up on outstanding actions;

Updating the Dialogue Tracker;

Maintaining contact lists; and

Keeping partner-engagement records current.

This area of work requires discretion, organization and the ability to distinguish significant decisions and action points from general discussion.

2. Partnership research and resource mobilization

The intern will research and map prospective private-sector and philanthropic partners, contributors and funding opportunities.

This will involve:

Identifying organizations whose priorities may align with WMO’s work;

Preparing institutional and partner profiles;

Monitoring developments in philanthropy and sustainable finance;

Researching private-sector engagement trends;

Comparing prospective contributors and opportunities;

Identifying potential areas of cooperation; and

Supporting the prioritization of partnership and resource-mobilization opportunities.

Candidates who can transform extensive research into short, decision-useful notes will be particularly well suited to this responsibility.

3. Due diligence and reputational-risk support

Before engaging an external organization, WMO must consider integrity, compliance and reputational risks.

The intern will assist by:

Conducting background research on companies, foundations and other prospective partners;

Reviewing relevant public information and databases;

Supporting the preparation of due-diligence reviews;

Assisting with reputational-risk assessments;

Documenting the information used in each review; and

Maintaining due-diligence records and trackers according to WMO procedures.

This responsibility is especially relevant for applicants interested in governance, compliance, corporate responsibility, legal research, ESG, ethical partnership management or reputational-risk analysis.

4. Support for the WMO Commons

The intern will contribute to the operationalization and promotion of the WMO Commons.

Expected assignments include:

Mapping prospective contributors and strategic partners;

Researching potential sources of financial and in-kind support;

Updating pitch materials;

Preparing frequently asked questions;

Developing presentations and communication products; and

Compiling background information for investor and contributor discussions.

The intern will learn how an international organization formulates a financing proposition and communicates it to different external audiences.

5. High-level event support

The intern will support Open Consultative Platform sessions and other major private-sector and philanthropic engagements.

Tasks may include:

Drafting or updating concept notes;

Supporting agenda development;

Researching participants;

Preparing briefing documents;

Maintaining logistical documentation;

Coordinating with participants;

Producing meeting reports; and

Tracking post-event actions.

The placement is therefore relevant to candidates interested in multistakeholder dialogue, international conferences, diplomacy, stakeholder coordination or strategic event management.

6. Strategic platforms and sector engagement

The intern will support WMO’s involvement in private-sector platforms, professional networks and global forums.

These may cover:

Sustainable finance;

Climate risk;

Technology and innovation;

Weather and climate services;

Water services; and

International development priorities.

The intern will monitor upcoming opportunities and prepare concise background papers or briefing materials as required.

7. Briefings, communications and knowledge management

The intern will help prepare written materials for senior-level engagements, including:

Official correspondence;

Briefing notes;

Talking points;

Presentations; and

Internal reports.

The intern will also maintain organized records of the Unit’s partnerships and activities and support information exchange with other WMO units and technical departments.

Additional related responsibilities may be assigned by the supervisor.

Educational Requirements

The vacancy calls for Master’s-level education in one of the following areas:

International relations;

International development;

Climate studies;

Business;

Economics;

Law or legal studies; or

Another related discipline.

WMO’s broader internship programme is intended for students and recent graduates. For this opening, candidates should be pursuing or have recently completed Master’s-level studies in a relevant field.

Applicants must describe their current degree status accurately. Incomplete studies should not be presented as a completed qualification.

Experience Requirements

No prior professional experience is required.

This allows candidates to use evidence from academic study, research assignments, dissertations, volunteering, student organizations or extracurricular activities.

Academic exposure to the following subjects would be advantageous:

International organizations;

Private-sector engagement;

Partnership development;

Resource mobilization;

Philanthropy;

Sustainable finance; or

Sustainability.

Candidates should not avoid applying merely because they have never worked for the United Nations. The announcement expressly states that previous professional experience is not required.

Required Skills

Applicants should demonstrate:

Strong research ability;

Strong professional writing skills;

Ability to work in a multicultural environment;

Close attention to detail; and

Ability to organize and manage multiple assignments simultaneously.

The duties also indicate the practical value of analytical writing, reliable recordkeeping, stakeholder awareness, professional communication, sound judgment and disciplined follow-up.

Language Requirements

Fluency in English is mandatory.

The assignment involves research, correspondence, meeting records, briefing notes, talking points, presentations and internal reports. Candidates should therefore be able to communicate clearly and accurately in professional English.

Knowledge of French is advantageous but is not identified as a mandatory requirement.

WMO’s general internship guidance also states that knowledge of another UN language—Arabic, Chinese, French, Russian or Spanish—is desirable, although this particular vacancy specifically highlights French. Review the official WMO internship conditions.

Learning and Professional Development

The official vacancy identifies hands-on learning in public-private engagement at the international level.

The selected intern can expect exposure to:

Partnership development within a UN specialized agency;

Private-sector and philanthropic engagement;

Contributor research and resource mobilization;

Due-diligence and reputational-risk processes;

High-level meeting and event coordination;

Strategic briefings and institutional communications;

Stakeholder databases and knowledge-management systems; and

Coordination among technical departments and external organizations.

WMO’s general internship programme also aims to help participants:

Understand WMO’s mandate;

Learn applicable organizational rules and guidance;

Develop an understanding of UN processes and policies;

Complete mandatory training;

Strengthen professional communication skills;

Understand the challenges of working in a multicultural organization; and

Build professional relationships and networks.

Stipend, Leave and Insurance

WMO’s published internship conditions state that interns recruited in Geneva receive CHF 1,500 per month.

For a six-month placement, this would equal CHF 9,000 over the complete internship before considering any special institutional funding or insurance-related addition.

Other published conditions include:

If an intern is funded by a WMO Member State or university, the allowance follows the sending organization’s policy;

Interns accrue 2.5 days of annual leave per month;

A six-month appointment would therefore accrue 15 days of annual leave;

Unused annual leave is not compensated when the internship ends;

Health and accident insurance is mandatory for interns based in Switzerland;

WMO recommends Swisscare for interns requiring sickness and accident insurance in Switzerland;

The intern completes the enrollment, while WMO covers the expense by adding the insurance cost to the monthly stipend;

Locally recruited interns who already have suitable insurance may opt out of Swisscare;

Interns are covered by the WMO Benefits Insurance Plan and Malicious Act Insurance Policy; and

If an intern is exceptionally required to travel for official work, accident and illness insurance applies to the travel.

These are WMO’s general programme conditions. The selected candidate’s appointment letter will remain the controlling document for exact financial and insurance arrangements.

Age, Diversity and Equal Opportunity

The vacancy states that candidates must be at least 18 years old.

It also mentions WMO’s statutory retirement age of 65 and requires an appointee to be able to complete the contract before reaching the mandatory age of separation.

WMO is committed to diversity and a balanced workforce. Qualified women and men, including persons with disabilities, are encouraged to apply.

No nationality, race, ethnicity or continental restriction is stated for this vacancy. WMO’s internship programme welcomes students and recent graduates from around the world.

Its general internship guidance describes the programme’s potential audience as:

Students approaching the end of their studies;

Students who need an internship to complete their studies;

Recent graduates;

Early-career candidates with less than two years of relevant experience;

Scholarship-supported interns; and

Candidates sponsored by governments, NGOs or academic institutions.

These general descriptions should be read together with the specific vacancy requirements, which state Master’s-level education, no prior experience requirement and a minimum age of 18.

How to Apply

Applications must be submitted through WMO’s online e-recruitment system. WMO does not accept applications by email or post.

Candidates should follow these steps:

Open the official vacancy page.

Sign in to an existing candidate account or create a new profile.

Complete all relevant profile sections, including education, language ability, academic projects and any work, volunteer or extracurricular experience.

Demonstrate Master’s-level study in an eligible discipline.

Provide evidence of research, writing, organization and multicultural teamwork.

Review the profile for accuracy, completeness and consistency.

Submit through the official portal only and avoid sending the application through multiple routes.

Apply before 22 September 2026, preferably well before the scheduled closing time.

The public vacancy text does not prescribe a fixed package of supporting documents. Candidates should follow the document prompts presented inside the recruitment portal.

WMO screens candidates using the information contained in their online profiles. Its official application guidance advises applicants to ensure that all information is complete and current before submission.

Practical Tips for a Competitive Application

These recommendations are based on the published responsibilities and are not additional eligibility conditions.

Connect academic work to the assignment

Candidates without extensive professional experience can use a thesis, capstone project, research paper or relevant coursework as evidence.

Strong subject areas may include:

Climate finance;

Sustainable development;

International organizations;

Corporate responsibility;

Philanthropy;

Partnership models;

Development economics;

Compliance; or

International law.

Demonstrate decision-focused research

Avoid merely stating that you possess research skills. Explain how you collected information, evaluated sources, identified risks or opportunities and converted findings into a clear output for a particular audience.

Provide evidence of professional writing

The role involves agendas, background notes, profiles, meeting summaries, briefing documents, talking points, presentations and reports.

Highlight assignments or activities requiring accurate, concise and audience-appropriate writing.

Show organization and follow-through

The Unit needs support across several events and partnership workstreams. Provide an example of managing competing deadlines, maintaining records, coordinating participants or tracking actions through completion.

Address due diligence and integrity

Applicants with exposure to legal research, compliance, ESG, reputational-risk analysis, corporate governance or ethical screening should make that connection explicit.

Academic experience can be relevant when it demonstrates careful evidence handling and balanced judgment.

Highlight multicultural experience

Evidence may come from international education, multicultural group projects, language learning, volunteer work, student associations or collaboration with people from different national and professional backgrounds.

Describe language ability accurately

English fluency is mandatory, while French is advantageous. Candidates should state their actual proficiency and support it with education, certification or practical experience where possible.

Selection Process

Only candidates in whom WMO has further interest will be contacted.

Shortlisted applicants may be required to:

Complete a written assessment;

Attend an interview; or

Participate in both stages.

Selected candidates will undergo relevant checks and must comply with WMO’s professional standards and principles.

WMO states that it does not tolerate harassment, sexual harassment, exploitation, discrimination or abuse of authority.

Important Clarification About the Probation Paragraph

The vacancy contains a generic paragraph under the heading “Probation” referring to possible renewal after a one-year probationary period, potentially extendable to two years.

That wording does not reconcile with:

The vacancy’s specific six-month contract duration; or

WMO’s general rule that internships last between three and twelve months.

The most reasonable interpretation is that this is standard recruitment text inadvertently included in the vacancy template. It should not be treated as an entitlement to a one- or two-year internship.

Applicants should rely on the stated six-month duration and seek clarification from the WMO Recruitment Helpdesk if this issue is material to their decision.

Recruitment Scam Warning

WMO does not charge an application, processing, interview, validation, appointment or training fee at any stage.

The Organization also states that it will not ask applicants for bank-account information during recruitment.

Candidates should apply only through the official recruitment portal and treat any payment request or unofficial offer claiming to guarantee selection as fraudulent.

Application Deadline and Official Link

Application deadline: 22 September 2026

Official application page: Apply through the WMO e-recruitment system

Because the application period is short, interested candidates should create or update their WMO profile and submit well before the closing day.

Conclusion

The WMO Internship 2026 in Geneva is a valuable opportunity for a Master’s-level student or recent graduate seeking experience at the intersection of climate action, international development, private-sector engagement, philanthropy and institutional partnerships.

The six-month placement combines substantive research with practical operational responsibilities. The selected intern will gain exposure to resource mobilization, reputational due diligence, high-level briefings, international events and collaboration across a United Nations specialized agency.

No previous professional experience is required, but candidates must demonstrate research ability, strong writing, attention to detail, multicultural teamwork and the capacity to manage several assignments simultaneously.

With a published Geneva stipend of CHF 1,500 per month and an application deadline of 22 September 2026, eligible applicants should review the official notice carefully and apply through WMO’s e-recruitment system without delay.

